// pfodWeb NOTE: GPIO14 tagged "button" - confirmed onboard SW2 pushbutton.
// Vendor's own espressif/arduino-esp32 variants/openkb/pins_arduino.h
// defines "static const uint8_t SW2 = 14;" (board-specific alias, not a
// generic name), and INEX's own OpenKB product page (inex.co.th/home/product/openkb/)
// independently confirms "Push Buttons: Two large tactile switches for
// user input" - matching the two SW1/SW2 aliases in the header.
// pfodWeb NOTE: GPIO16 (aliased as both SW1 and, contradictorily, as
// LED_BUILTIN in the vendor pins_arduino.h) is left untagged: GPIO16
// falls in this chip's PSRAM-reserved 16-17 exclusion range and has no
// chipGpios entry at all, so re-adding it would reverse a pre-existing
// chip-level exclusion - out of scope for this task. See
// boardsDetails/esp32/esp32/openkb/notes.txt for the full writeup of the
// SW1/LED_BUILTIN naming contradiction.
// pfodWeb NOTE: GPIO17 (BT_LED in vendor pins_arduino.h) is also left
// untagged for the same reason - it falls in the same chip-level PSRAM
// 16-17 exclusion range.
// pfodWeb NOTE: GPIO2 (WIFI_LED), GPIO12 (IOT_LED) and GPIO15 (NTP_LED)
// are real named status LEDs per the vendor header, and INEX's own IoT
// tutorial (doc.inex.co.th/openkb_kidbright_iot/) independently confirms
// a "WiFi status LED" and "IoT status LED" light up on the physical
// board - but no vendor schematic/source found confirms active-high vs
// active-low wiring for any of them, so all three are deliberately left
// untagged (judgment call - see notes.txt for human review).
